The mining industry vowed not to oppose a renewed attempt by an opposition MP to bring greater transparency in its operations overseas Thursday.
��The momentum is pushing this way,�� said Ben Chalmers, vice-president of sustainable development at the Mining Association of Canada, which represents the country��s biggest miners.
Related U.S. raises bar on U.S. raises bar on mining disclosure ; McKay would see Canada follow
The extractive business, including oil and gas producers, is facing a brave new world of corporate accountability after the U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission passed the much-awaited Amendment 1504 of the Dodd-Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act, which will require them to disclose all payments to governments in an annual filing.
��Our members are very supportive in principle about this type of disclosure,�� said Chalmers. ��It gives investors another level of information they can use to make responsible investment decisions.��
The new rules are essentially an anti-corruption measure, designed to bring transparency around how deals are done in resource-rich, but government-weak, regions.
��It provides communities with the information to uphold their governments to account and raise the level of governance,�� said Chalmers.
Many of the amendment��s details have yet to be defined – including the commission��s definition of a mining ��project�� – so Chalmers cautioned that the industry may have some qualms down the road.
��It will be important to make the requirements as efficient as possible to keep the burden as low as possible,�� he said.
Many of Canada��s biggest miners are cross-listed on the New York Stock Exchange, so when the rules come into effect in September 2013, they��ll have to be compliant.
There will also likely be a cascade effect among smaller firms who are seeking to one day list in the U.S. or who are hoping to be acquired.
��One of the ways that smaller companies will add value is through (corporate social responsibility) best practices,�� said Chalmers. ��I think it will make it easier for larger companies to take comfort in the fact that if they buy into a project, they know they��ll have an easier time developing it.��
That point may be moot if a Liberal MP has his way.
John McKay, who tried to bring greater accountability to mining practices overseas in a failed 2010 attempt through a private members�� bill, is working on new legislation that will mirror Amendment 1504.
��In some respects, it would be very parallel to this bill,�� McKay said Wednesday, who is still finalizing the proposal��s details and plans to release it in the fall.
McKay��s original bill, which surprised many by reaching second reading before being narrowly defeated, would have given federal government agencies rules around investing in mining companies with bad reputations.
The new bill comes at the issue from a different angle, by forcing the disclosure of all payments to governments to a regulatory body.
McKay��s office has been in discussions with the House of Commons lawyers and a team of civil society groups that helped design the U.S. legislation on a Canadian version throughout the summer, said McKay. Those NGOs include Revenue Watch, Oxfam and Publish What You Pay.
But in a marked departure from past animosity between McKay and the industry, the Mining Association of Canada says it has been providing McKay with advice on the bill.
��John��s relationship with us is different than it was then,�� said Chalmers, referring to the old legislation. ��It��s much more positive and cooperative, which has put us in a good position to work with him to improve his bill.��
When McKay releases his new proposal, the association said it won��t contest it – a change from the last dust-up.
��We��ve decided we won��t support it but we won��t oppose it,�� said Chalmers. ��And that��s really not reflective of the topic at all. We��re really supportive of transparency. We just don��t want to wade into the politics of choosing to support or not a private member��s bill.��
Taking a hands-off approach over a bill that will have major consequences on its members may seem odd for the association, but the political dynamic this time around is much different.{g}
