The federal court system is supposed to be a place where bamboozlers and bad guys get their comeuppance.
Unfortunately, it appears that a man who pretended to be a lawyer turned the tables on the court system.
Howard O. Kieffer is an ex-con who represented at least 16 clients in 10 federal court jurisdictions, including Colorado, according to a Denver Post story written by reporter Felisa Cardona. He does not appear to be a licensed attorney anywhere in the country.
It seems that Kieffer took advantage of a system that, at least in Colorado, relies on lawyers to tell the truth about their professional backgrounds when they ask for permission to practice. It��s a quaint idea and one that needs to be revamped.
Each week, about 15 or 20 lawyers apply to practice in Colorado��s federal district court. Generally, the court administrator��s office reviews the applications to make sure they are complete, but the information is not typically verified, said Greg Langham, Colorado��s U.S. District Court clerk.
��We get way too many of those, normally,�� he told us.
The system relies upon officers of the court being who they say they are. Langham said it typically works out just fine. In the five years he has been clerk, he said he knows of no other incident similar to that of the Kieffer situation.
While that may be, we still think it��s a good idea for a court clerk to at least check whether applicants are licensed lawyers. Such checks ought to be quickly and easily accomplished.
The state court system in Colorado four years ago began verifying lawyers�� credentials, according to The Post��s story. John Gleason, attorney regulation counsel for the Colorado Supreme Court, said the process has snagged people who are not licensed lawyers or who have been suspended.
It makes you wonder what may have gone undetected in the federal system.
