ANAHEIM - Ever since the Angels changed their name, team officials have sworn that merchandise bearing "Los Angeles" would not officially be available.
Not anymore.
The first "Los Angeles Angels" T-shirts popped up on Major League Baseball's Web site just one day after the team snagged the American League West championship.
MLB promised to pull the T-shirt off its merchandise line Wednesday night, saying it doesn't sanction items reading "Los Angeles Angels." Officials planned to look into how the mistake happened, said Carmine Tiso, an MLB spokesman.
Angels spokesman Tim Mead said the team had nothing to do with the sale, as MLB licenses the shirts. The team has used the "Big A," "Angels Baseball" or "Angels" on merchandise since the Jan. 3 name change.
"There would be concern there. This is not something produced by the Angels, so we'll look into it," Mead said.
The new shirts irked lawyers for Anaheim, which is suing the team for becoming the Los Angeles Angels of Anaheim. The city claims the new name breaks the lease requiring "Anaheim" to be in the team name.
